te datagram; a\0A *                      value of \5Cc 1 means that, if space permits,\0A *                      multiple handshake messages (including CCS) belonging to\0A *                      a single flight may be packed within a single datagram.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           This is enabled by default and should only be disabled\0A *                 for test purposes, or if datagram packing causes\0A *                 interoperability issues with peers that don't support it.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Allowing datagram packing reduces the network load since\0A *                 there's less overhead if multiple messages share the same\0A *                 datagram. Also, it increases the handshake efficiency\0A *                 since messages belonging to a single datagram will not\0A *                 be reordered in transit, and so future message buffering\0A *                 or flight retransmission (if no buffering is used) as\0A *                 means to deal with reordering are needed less frequently.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Application records are not affected by this option and\0A *                 are currently always sent in separate datagrams.\0A *\0A */\0Avoid mbedtls_ssl_set_datagram_packing(mbedtls_ssl_context *ssl,\0A                                      unsigned allow_packing);\0A\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief          Set retransmit timeout values for the DTLS handshake.\0A *                 (DTLS only, no effect on TLS.)\0A *\0A * \5Cparam conf     SSL configuration\0A * \5Cparam min      Initial timeout value in milliseconds.\0A *                 Default: 1000 (1 second).\0A * \5Cparam max      Maximum timeout value in milliseconds.\0A *                 Default: 60000 (60 seconds).\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           Default values are from RFC 6347 section 4.2.4.1.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote           The 'min' value should typically be slightly above the\0A *                 expected round-trip time to your peer, plus whatever time\0A *                 it take