@.str62075 = private global { i64, i64, [14625 x i8] } { i64 -1, i64 14624, [14625 x i8] c"/// The runtime primitive. Intercepted by `cg_runtime_fn`, which keys\0A/// on the bare name and so does not care that it now lives in a\0A/// module rather than in the prelude.\0Alet os_time_raw (ignored: Bool): Int = 0\0A\0A/// Seconds since the Unix epoch, from the system clock.\0A///\0A/// Seconds, and an `Int`, because that is what every platform's\0A/// `time()` agrees on. Anything richer (a calendar date, a\0A/// timezone, a formatted string) is a library on top of this and\0A/// not a runtime concern; the primitive is the part a program\0A/// cannot write for itself.\0Apub let now (): Int = os_time_raw(true)\0A\0A// --- Durations and clocks ---\0A//\0A// `Time.now()` above stays SECONDS, and every calendar function\0A// below stays arithmetic on it. Changing it would break every\0A// caller and every documented `iso8601` answer to buy precision\0A// that belongs in a separate type anyway.\0Aextern \22C\22 {\0A    fn time_now_millis() -> Int;\0A    fn time_monotonic_nanos() -> Int;\0A    fn time_sleep_nanos(n: Int) -> Int;\0A}\0A\0A/// A length of time, held as NANOSECONDS.\0A///\0A/// A real type rather than an `Int` of milliseconds, because a\0A/// bare number does not say which unit it is and every caller has\0A/// to agree by convention. `#7` will want timeouts, `Http` will\0A/// want them, `Terminal` will want them, and the alternative is\0A/// each module growing its own `timeout_ms: Int` that cannot be\0A/// passed to the others.\0A///\0A/// The field is PRIVATE: a `Duration` is built through a\0A/// constructor that names its unit, which is the whole point of\0A/// having the type. Nanoseconds internally because a signed 64-bit\0A/// count of them spans ~292 years, which is more than any duration\0A/// needs, and because losing sub-millisecond precision would make\0A/// the type useless for the timing code that wants it most.\0Apub struct Duration { nanos: Int }